{# PM·A — THE GRID. Positions across, permissions down, a box at every
   intersection, so "who may delete an area" is one row read left to right.

   Every position added is another column, and past a dozen the table is a
   horizontal scroll nobody reads accurately — which is the cost, and the
   reason B rather than A is the shipped direction.

   THE GRID IS READ-ONLY. Every other direction edits one position at a time and
   can therefore post one position's permissions; a grid would have to post a
   whole matrix, and a save that rewrites nine positions at once is a save
   nobody can undo confidently. It links each column to its own editor instead. #}
{% import '@UhifadhiTeam/positions/_bits.html.twig' as pm %}

<div class="c" data-pm="a">
    <span class="tab">The grid<span class="src">&middot; every position, every permission</span></span>
    <p class="use" style="margin-top:2px">One row per permission and one column per position, so a single row
    answers <b>who may do this</b>. It reads across rather than down, which no other direction does. <b>It does
    not edit:</b> a save here would rewrite every position at once, and that is not an action anybody can undo
    confidently &mdash; each column heading is a link to that position&rsquo;s own checklist.</p>

    {% if positions is empty %}
        <p class="use"><b>The grid opens on nothing at all</b>, which is exactly the weakness that made B the
        shipped direction: there are no positions yet, so there are no columns. <a href="{{ path('team_positions') }}">Make the first one.</a></p>
    {% else %}
        <div class="pm-scroll">
            <table class="tbl pm-grid">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        {# THE PINNED COLUMN. `.pm-rowh` is what makes the permission
                           column stick while the positions scroll under it, and what
                           gives it the width the description needs — a grid whose row
                           header scrolls away is a grid of anonymous ticks. #}
                        <th class="pm-rowh"><span class="lbl" style="margin:0">permission</span></th>
                        {# A COLUMN HEAD STACKS: department, then name, then who holds it.
                           Written inline as a `.qual` it was one long nowrap run per
                           column, and eight of them ran into each other and off the
                           right of the card — the grid's whole head was unreadable.
                           `.pm-colh` is the design's own block for this and bounds the
                           column's width, which is what lets eight positions fit. #}
                        {% for position in positions %}
                            {% set held = holders[position.uuidString]|default(0) %}
                            <th>
                                <a class="pm-colh" href="{{ path('team_positions', {position: position.uuidString}) }}">
                                    <span class="pm-dept"><i class="dot"></i>{{ position.department ? position.department.name : 'no department' }}</span>
                                    <b>{{ position.name }}</b>
                                    <span class="held">{% if held %}{{ held }} {{ held == 1 ? 'person' : 'people' }}{% else %}nobody yet{% endif %}</span>
                                </a>
                            </th>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {% for umbrella, permissions in grouped %}
                        <tr class="tm-bandrow"><td colspan="{{ positions|length + 1 }}"><div class="tm-band">
                            <b>{{ umbrella }}</b>
                            {% if permissions[0].isCore %}
                                <span class="pm-by core"><i></i>the host</span>
                            {% else %}
                                <span class="pm-by {{ permissions[0].source }}"><i></i>{{ moduleNames[permissions[0].source]|default(permissions[0].source) }}</span>
                            {% endif %}
                        </div></td></tr>
                        {# THE TWO MARKS THAT ARE PROPERTIES OF THE PERMISSION, not of a
                           layout: the one core permission that destroys something, and
                           the one that grants authority over other people. B carries
                           them on `.pm-row`; the grid carries them on the row. #}
                        {% for permission in permissions %}
                            <tr class="{{ permission.value ends with '.delete' ? 'destructive' : '' }}{{ permission.value == 'team.manage' ? ' elevated' : '' }}">
                                <td class="pm-rowh">
                                    <div class="pm-name">
                                        <b>{{ permission.label }}</b>
                                        <span class="perm">{{ permission.value }}</span>
                                        <em>{{ permission.description }}</em>
                                    </div>
                                </td>
                                {# THE INTERSECTION, in the design's own vocabulary: `.pm-box` is the
                                   whole-cell mark and the `<i>` inside it is the drawn box. It is a
                                   span and not a button here because THIS DIRECTION IS READ-ONLY —
                                   the ruled deviation for A and C — so `.pm-ro` takes back the hover
                                   and the pointer a clickable cell would offer and cannot honour. #}
                                {% for position in positions %}
                                    <td>
                                        <span class="pm-box pm-ro{{ permission.value in position.permissionValues ? ' on' : '' }}">
                                            <i>{{ pm.icon_tick() }}</i>
                                        </span>
                                    </td>
                                {% endfor %}
                            </tr>
                        {% endfor %}
                    {% endfor %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
</div>
